{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The dev-tool MCP server that could read your cloud credentials</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A developer wired up a local MCP server for a code-formatting tool — nothing more than "read this file, format it, write it back." They launched it the easy way: spawn the server as a child process, pass it the same environment the terminal already had. It worked immediately, so nobody looked closer.
    </p>
    <p>
      That same terminal session also had <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> and a database connection string sitting in its environment, left over from an unrelated task earlier that day. The formatting server never asked for them, never used them — but nothing stopped it from reading <code>process.env.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> if its code (or a compromised dependency inside it) ever did.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A child process launched without an explicit <code>env</code> option inherits the entire parent environment by default — that's how <code>child_process.spawn</code> (and equivalents in most languages) work, not an MCP-specific behavior. For a local MCP server, that means every environment variable in the developer's shell, unrelated secrets included, is available to a process whose actual job might be "format a file" or "list open GitHub issues."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a least-privilege violation baked into the default plumbing, not a deliberate design choice anyone made — full inheritance is what happens when nobody scopes anything, because it's the path of least resistance for getting a local server running quickly. It's exactly the kind of gap that matters more for MCP servers than for typical child processes, because MCP servers are explicitly designed to be extended with third-party tools and dependencies a developer may not have fully audited.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Pass exactly the env vars the server actually needs, nothing else</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of handing a spawned MCP server the whole environment, build an explicit object with only the variables it actually needs — a formatting tool needs <code>PATH</code> to find its binaries, maybe nothing else; a server that calls a specific API needs that API's token and nothing about your cloud provider, your database, or any other service's secrets.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Concretely, that means constructing the <code>env</code> passed to the spawn call as an allowlist rather than a passthrough — copy in <code>PATH</code> and a small set of OS variables most processes need to start correctly (things like <code>HOME</code>), then add only the specific keys the server was actually built to consume. The official MCP TypeScript SDK's stdio client transport does exactly this by default via a curated <code>getDefaultEnvironment()</code> allowlist, rather than passing through the full parent environment — that default exists specifically to prevent the leak this case describes, so a hand-rolled spawn wrapper that skips it is opting back into the less-safe behavior.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Scoping is a blast-radius decision, not a correctness one — the formatting server was never going to misuse the AWS key under normal operation, but "normal operation" isn't the threat model. A supply-chain compromise in one of the server's own dependencies, a prompt-injected instruction that gets the agent to ask the tool to dump its environment, or simply a bug that logs <code>process.env</code> during a crash are all real vectors, and every one of them is neutralized by a secret that was never in the child process's environment to begin with, regardless of how the compromise happened.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The postmortem that found nothing, because there was nothing to find</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A different team ran a security review across their local dev tooling and found the same full-environment-inheritance pattern in three separate MCP servers — a linter, a git helper, and an internal API client. None had ever leaked anything. But none of them had ever been asked to prove they couldn't, either, and "no incident yet" isn't the same claim as "not exploitable."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      They rewrote all three spawn configs to pass an explicit <code>env</code> object: the linter got <code>PATH</code> only, the git helper got <code>PATH</code> plus <code>GIT_SSH_COMMAND</code>, and the internal API client got <code>PATH</code> plus its one scoped API token — nothing else, deliberately including nothing about cloud credentials, database URLs, or any other service's secrets that happened to live in a developer's shell.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The real value showed up a month later, when the internal API client picked up a new third-party dependency that turned out to have a known vulnerability allowing arbitrary file reads within the process's own permissions. Because that process's environment held exactly one secret — its own scoped token — the actual exposure from the vulnerability was "attacker can read the API client's own token," not "attacker can read every secret that happened to be in the developer's terminal that day." Scoping the environment didn't prevent the vulnerability; it capped what the vulnerability could reach.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

