{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The allowlist itself needs to be per-server, not global</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Don't build one shared "safe env" object and reuse it for every MCP server you spawn — a git helper genuinely needs <code>GIT_SSH_COMMAND</code>, a formatting tool doesn't, and giving every server the union of what any server might need quietly recreates the over-broad-inheritance problem one variable at a time.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      If a server's config format supports declaring its own required env keys (many MCP client configs do, alongside <code>command</code> and <code>args</code>), treat that declaration as the actual allowlist source, not documentation — deriving the runtime allowlist from the same declaration a human reads keeps the two from drifting apart the way a hand-maintained duplicate list eventually would.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This same reasoning extends past MCP: any local tool that spawns a subprocess with access to a broader environment than its stated job requires is making the same trade, whether it's a build tool, a linter, a git hook, or a language server. Treat "does this child process need <code>env: process.env</code>, or does it need three specific variables" as a question worth asking on every spawn call in a codebase, not a special case reserved for the MCP servers that happen to be labeled "agent tooling."
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

